Peter Petrakis <voodoo@alphadriven.org> wrote:

> I'm pretty sure the mozilla RPMS have debugging code/symbols
> enabled. That will slow down the thing signifigantly. Try recompiling
> without debug and something like -O2 and compare your results.

IIRC, it used to be this way, but it isn't anymore.  The mozilla spec
file now gives --enable-optimize and --disable-debug options to
configure.  These options have been given in the source RPMS since
mozilla-0.9.1, at least (the oldest spec file I have laying around).

By default, --enable-optimize sets MOZ_OPTIMIZE_FLAGS to "-O", which
most things get compiled with.  Some stuff gets compiled with -O2
(some of the straight C code), though, and a couple places even use
-O3.

But it still might be interesting to bump MOZ_OPTIMIZE_FLAGS to -O2 or
otherwise fiddle with the compiler flags some (-fomit-frame-pointer?
-funroll-loops?  etc.).  Or build with ccc/cxx.

Cheers,
Craig




