On Wednesday 10 April 2002 04:41 pm, Peter Watkinson wrote:
> A couple of other questions. Is wireless networking
> safe? 

If you set it up right.  I have two Linksys WAP11 for access points, 
Linksys WCF11 (compact flash) for a Sharp Zaurus, Linksys WMP11 
(pcmicia cards), and a USR 2410.  The network cards are all running on 
Linux boxes and I don't have any problems.

To lock down the access points you should use WEP encription along with 
MAC address filtering.  The MAC address filtering will keep people from 
getting on your access points.  WEP encription will keep people who 
have their wireless card in promiscuous mode from snooping your 
traffic.  The problem is most access points will require you to use 
windows with USB (98, ME, 2k or xp) to completely configure the access 
point.  There are projects to control the linksys access points via 
snmp on Linux, but none of them allow MAC address updates.

Other than that, if you have pcmcia and the wireless drivers most linux 
installs just need you to plug in the card and set some minor setup 
stuff.  If you are using dhcp you can probably get away with just 
plugging the card in.

As far as range you are talking line of site.  I loose the connection if 
it goes through 5+ internal walls or 2+ brick walls.  And it definately 
doesn't go through concrete floors.  Far enough and a person walking 
between you and the access point will drop your connection.

- mark arrasmith

