At 16:20 10/04/2002, Collin Baillie sent this up the stick:

>I have a friend's PWS500a. It currently has an 850MB IDE disk and AlphaBIOS.
>I'd like to run Linux on it, and have confirmed it switches to SRM ok.

You could use AlphaBIOS to load milo, that way you wouln't need a SCSI 
controller that SRM supports.  That being said, apparently the IDE 
interface on the Miata sucks - I wouldn't know, I have only ever used SRM 
(I have a 500au)

>I'd like to install a SCSI disk, but it seems not to have SCSI anywhere on
>the m/board and only has 1 PCI card - a Matrox II

Fair 'nuff

>So I'd like to know which SCSI cards will work with SRM as a bootable
>controller. (I've been told Qlogic 1020/1040b and 53c8xx should work, but
>have read that 53c895 don't [under Tru64 anyway]).

Correct, but I'm pretty sure the 53c895 have a driver once the kernel is 
loaded.  I have successfully booted off Tekram SCSI cards, which use the 
Symbios version of the 53c8xx chip, and have read somewhere that the 
Advansys cards use the same chipset.


>I have an Adaptec 3940,
>which boots perfectly for WinNT on the box, but I have confirmed that SRM
>doesn't even see it.

But once the kernel is loaded (off the IDE?) Linux will be able to use the 
Adaptec.

>  Also I saw mention of an a-series -> au-series upgrade
>which contained a 2MB cache module. Am I correct to assume this would be for
>the CPU (L2/3 cache)? Would it effect performance seriously by *not* having
>it?

Apparently, having the BCache is a Good Thing, some people report 
significant usability increases.

Cheers,
Rob




