On Thursday 11 April 2002 05:22 am, Peter Watkinson wrote:
> What is VNC?

I thought a throw in a couple of interesting vnc links along with 
everyone else.  You can export your :0 X display using vnc.  It is a 
version of vnc called X0rfbserver.

http://hexonet.de/software/rfb/
http://hexonet.de/software/x0rfbserver/index.html

I'm using it for labs and classroom computers where I need to display 
student's screens on the data projector if needed.  VNC is really cool 
for stateful X sessions as well.

> Also can you connect an Ipaq to a wireless network? Does
> it require a special wireless network card? How did you install Linux
> on the Ipaq?

(I'll apologize up front for the offtopic stuff)

I probably shouldn't say this . . . But, if anyone doesn't have an Ipaq 
and is interested in a Linux handheld I really recommend the Sharp 
Zaurus (I have a developers version).  It is binary compatible with the 
Linux installed Ipaq's and has some really cool expansion features.  
You don't have to purchase an expansion sleave, you can just get CF 
cards (wireless networking, 1Gig IBM microdrives, cameras, etc) and use 
the SD slot for extra storage as well.  And it has a builtin keyboard 
and face buttons that you can assign any app to.  Or as a programmer 
take over the button's functions for your app.

If Compaq releases a developer or consumer version of the Ipaq with 
Linux/Qtopia I'd look at an Ipaq then.  Don't know why they don't.  If 
the Zaurus takes off, being able to use the same apps as them would be 
a big plus.

Anyway sorry for being off topic.

- mark arrasmith

