On Thu, 18 Apr 2002, YujiMiyahara wrote:

> I did not check the source codes, but I contacted Gaussian Inc. for my
> problems and got an aswer that LINUX Alpha was not a 64 bit OS and
> they were trying to persuade Comaq to make it 64 bit.  As a remedy they
> suggested to use Tru64 UNIX instead.  I just trusted their answer as the
> Gaussian98 developer.  That is all.

As I'm sure everyone else has summarily stated, Linux on Alpha is 64-bit,
contrary to what they've told you.  I could go into history (BLADE was
32-bit cross-compiled for a bit), but I'll spare the long diatribe :-P

> If the fault is solely on Gaussian98, I would like to change the source
> codes.
> Will you teach me where to change?

If they claim it compiles on Tru64 and works, then start by looking for
any conditionals in the source that are Tru64-specific.  Also, you may
want to double check that they don't lump Alpha Linux in with i386 Linux
in other conditionals (ie. "#ifdef linux" will test true on both, when you
may not want that to happen).  There are numerous other things to look
for, but they are too numerous to mention here.  You can contact me
off-list if you'd like me to take a quick look at the code for obvious
erroneous assumptions (if that doesn't violate their license, that is...it
may be better to work with them to solve the problems).

According to the web page, they claim SuSE support on Alpha, so,
personally, I'd throw the problem back at them.

C




