To all interested parties:

	I've been playing with FreeBSD on my LX164, there are some
things I like about it, some I don't.  When I was looking at gentoo
linux, I noticed they were adopting a bsd-style ports setup, but they
don't support alpha...

	One thing that struck me about bsd is that the ports system
allows patches to be applied to stock source to make the package run
better on a specific os/arch type.

	When we had our suggestion list, one thing I noticed was a lack
of alpha-specific things in the stock code of packages.  (ie, not using
the full potential of the alpha in the code.)

	I also find the Linux kernel to be extremely flexible and
powerful, one big advantage it has over BSD.  Also the user base for
alpha seems to be stronger in linux (from my personal experience) on the
alpha than BSD.

	I think it would be a good idea if a group of alpha linux users
developed an alpha distribution of linux using a bsd-style ports system
(and any type of alpha-specific patches we wanted to apply) and focus on
the specific needs we as alpha linux users have.  We could take the best
of all the current distributions out there, and build on them and make
linux on alpha extremely fast, reliable, and secure.  (I'm not meaning
to offend any die-hard people about their distro of choice, just
proposing a new idea)

	Does that interest anyone on this list?

Yours truly,
Ted Goodridge
tedgoodridgejr@acm.org
  





