That's not a problem with RPM, but the packager. In fact, RedHat
and Mandrake are substantially compatible and binary RPMS that I
make for Icarus Verilog or gtkwave tend to install on both.

And if they don't, the src rpm will.

The issues you are complaining about are more a matter of the
directory structure that the product at hand assumes. Debian,
for example, can get picky and slightly odd about those sorts
of things. Early on I had to make some changes to Icarus Verilog
to make it "Debian Compatible."

I like rpms because they not only handle the install, but the
un-install and interdependencies. The installation is usually the
least of my worries. It also reverse maps files to packages, a
very convenient feature at times when you are trying to figure
out what the heck /etc/foo.bar is about.

Just feel glad that *you* don't have to write Windows installers.
Ugh.

