Heh, I wasn't trying to start an argument on whether RPM's are bad or
not, I just really like the ports system, and the ability to compile
fetched and fresh source for large projects with a single make, make
install.  Plus I've seen that ports system has a builtin safeguard,
OpenBSD calls it 'faking' the installation, to make sure it will install
properly.

What impressed me personally about the ports system is how up to date it
is, and it how much easier it is to compile _from source_.  I'm not
really interested in binary packages (yes I'm insane).  But working
backwards for source dependencies has been the bane of my existence.

What I'm really interested in with my initial post is whether or not
this is of interest to any of *YOU*.  Call it 'market research'. :)
Gentoo linux seems to be taking off, but there is no alpha port.

Redhat, Suse, and debian are all great distros, but it seems like alpha
is always last on the priority list.  In my dream scenario, this
proposed distro would focus on alpha and only alpha, and getting it to
work on all alpha.

"Of the alpha, by the alpha, and for the alpha"

Redhat 7.2 isn't going to be ported to all the old alphas out there, and
judging from the posts made to this list about it, I think there is a
need for a distro that focuses squarely on making the alpha truly what
it can be.

Just a thought.
Ted Goodridge

