On Fri, 19 Apr 2002, Peter Petrakis wrote:

> Being open is what I think what made RPM part of LSB
> and not DPKG.

Not really.  There are quite a few political reasons (both within the
RH-based dist community and inside Debian) for RPM to be part of LSB, but
I won't get into them as this isn't the appropriate forum for such a
discussion.  Also, the fact that RH holds a majority of the "market
share" (is there such a thing? has anyone really done a study?) made the
biggest difference.  In short, the package managers weren't necessarily
weighed entirely upon their technical merits, from what I can gather.  Any
RPM vs. dpkg debate in the LSB context is really moot anyway since
accomplish the same goals, just differently.

> concatinating a patch to the existing gz file of patches is the minimum
> of effort required to update a package. Then you get to something like X windows
> that has a seperate patch tree that is compiled into a single gz when the
> package
> is built. The later is much more difficult to manage.

Actually, it's easier to manage.  I keep the binutils maintained using a
separate patch dir and my turnaround time for a new upstream source is
less than 25% of the time that it took to do one before I went to a patch
dir.  It all depends on the package, however.  Smaller packages with few
patches don't really need that "the arm patch is safe for every arch but
powerpc" functionality in their build scripts.

> RPM has whats called a 'buildroot' which is a temporary location to which the
> fresh
> build is installed to. So you can make sure things are installed where they
> should
> 'and' with the proper sed/awk magic (thanks KDE) you can generate an authorative
> file
> list of what is to be installed. Which is then integrated into RPM. As for deb,
> I just
> havnt run into that side of it yet though im pretty sure they do something
> similar.

We do something similar.  In reality, VERY few people who generate RPMs
actually use the buildroot to verify much of anything.  What buildroot is
really there for are packages that save their final linking steps for when
you do a 'make install'.  Debian installs to a subdir within the debian/
directory in a common installation directory.  Then, the developer tools
take over and split the package up into multiple packages (if there are
such a thing for what you're compiling).  It's quite nice and nearly
automatic.

> > What impressed me personally about the ports system is how up to date it
> > is, and it how much easier it is to compile _from source_.  I'm not
> > really interested in binary packages (yes I'm insane).  But working
> > backwards for source dependencies has been the bane of my existence.

To reply a bit to the original author a bit, I think that the lack of
binary distribution is what has kept *BSD's install-bases below linux
(aside from the buzzword-motivated managers and press, that is).  I'm not
cracking on the ports system at all.  In fact, I like it, but personally
don't prefer it since I don't really relish the idea of compiling things
from source on very old/slow machines.

Also, there are several packages that, for better or worse, are definitely
NOT the most up-to-date on any *BSD system (including bug-fixes).  This is
not an accusation or a bad thing necessarily, just an observation of fact.

> debian unstable is pretty current. Im from the school of "aint broke dont fix
> it"
> so I may be missing a few things. Though I admit X isnt as up to date as I would
> like.

I truly believe, at this point, that the "if it ain't broke, don't fix
it" crowd is seriously outnumbered by the "we MUST have the latest
version, damn the bugs" throngs, unfortunately.  It almost seems like
software development is expected to actually move faster than hardware
development these days and fixing bugs is an annoyance rather than the
primary goal.

> maintainers of major packages i.e. gcc, binutils, X, and kernel absolutley
> MUST get fixes into upstream sources or else their patch tree becomes more
> than just a few fixes with some ditro tweaks but a whole different animal.

True.  Many of us have taken the stance that, if it's not a dist-specific
fix (i.e. installation directory targets, etc) and the maintainer doesn't
have a ton of time to follow-up with upstream, we would rather wait until
the patches get accepted upstream before including them in the package.

> Sending the patches to the package maintainers of the 'big three' ;-) has
> a better chance of getting the fix into upstream sources. I will not
> rank them.

Please don't :-P

> > Redhat, Suse, and debian are all great distros, but it seems like alpha
> > is always last on the priority list.  In my dream scenario, this
> > proposed distro would focus on alpha and only alpha, and getting it to
> > work on all alpha.

Again to the original author...if it seems like alpha is last on the list,
what have you tried to do to improve that?  I don't want to blame the
users (really, I'm not doing that now...keep reading), but there is only
so much some of us can do while there are still only 24 hours in a day and
we have day jobs.  Also, have you tried to express your concerns with the
distribution folks of your chosen dist?  Considering that I haven't
received an email from you, I suspect that you haven't really had too many
complaints with Debian :-)

> A long time ago Barret Lyon had a similar idea, he was a BSD guy. We where
> going to call it ALO ports and distribute it on the ftp site and CVS. What made
> the project get pushed to the side was the shear amount of legwork needed to
> maintain more than a few packages. Between two/three guys it would have been
> futile. That and the areas of expertise required to maintain those sources
> that are signifigant was lacking. So... If this is going to happen there will
> need to be some experts who are willing to maintain upstream sources and feed
> patches to the maintainers.

You need more than that, believe me.  At face value, it seems like anyone
can make a distribution, which is pretty much true, but maintaining that
dist is f*ing brutal.  There have been times when there were only three of
us working on Debian Alpha and I guarantee you that we really didn't get
any sleep during those times (I'm not sure my wife remembered my name
around then since she never saw me).  Also, keep in mind that you'll need
a variety of expertise to make a good dist: not only good application
folks that know their way around several programming languages, but you'll
also need some really good kernel people (that means someone who Alan Cox
will actually pay attention to if they send in patches).  Good luck trying
to pool that talent for just about any project these days...you have a
better chance of herding cats into a box.  I don't want to dissuade
anyone, but you should walk into it with your eyes open.

> There are more problems inheritant in this simple notion. Like whos going to
> test
> these newly patched sources. The expert may be that but they are not the author.
> I'm going to stop now :-).

Me too :-P

> Or released at all :-). Oh, RH users. I'm gonna fix the stupid GPM bug in
> debian.
> I've used GPM/X on RH for FIVE YEARS and have never had a problem with it and
> then to
> switch distros (debian) and find it to not work AT ALL. I saw some
> RH->debian->RH users
> mention this 'feature' a few months ago.

Thanks.  Me thinks gpm is evil, personally, so I never use it.

> I agree but alas there is only 24 hours in a single day and sleep is a
> requirement.

Sleep?  What's that?  :-)

