Hey thanks to all who bothered to take this all into account.

First I'd like to state that I agree with RPM's being adopted more on
RedHat's muscle than merit.  It's one of the reasons many of my friends
stay away from redhat. (In fact for many it's the only reason to stay
away from RedHat.  They feel that as users, RPM's give them little
freedom to work with)

I understand this will be a huge undertaking.  In response to the
comment that users need to contribute more, I fully agree.  Alpha seems
to be placed on the back burner a lot, and I want to see what I can do
to change that.

My biggest concern now for the alpha platform is that the distributions
show their age on the alpha much sooner than for x86.  For example, I
had a not very fun time getting some libs to compile because they were
written from an x86 standpoint.  Peter has stated that there is not
enough time in the day to port something as massive as GNOME regularly.

I honestly don't care if RPM's or ports are used, as long as its very
easy to compile from source.  I know binary distros are necessary for
the slower machines, as someone pointed out.

My biggest question was posted to the list to see if there would be
support for such a distro, focused only on the alpha.  It could be based
on any number of major distros, just with all the developers poised on
porting libs, proggies, etc to the alpha.

I don't know how long you guys have been waiting on redhat 7.2 for the
alpha, but I'm about tired of waiting.  I got an email from the Suse
alpha team, and they said Suse alpha *may* be released 'sometime q3/q4'.
(To answer one post, I have contacted Suse numerous times, took 3 weeks
to get that much out of them!)

I'm an end user when it comes to Linux, and I'm trying to dive into some
documentation to change that.  I'm studying CS at the University of
Oklahoma, but as of now my skills are limited.

I don't know how this idea hit me, perhaps it's the virus talking (or
the pain killers.)  I just thought that alpha doesn't get the respect it
deserves and there are plenty of people on this list who could band
together and make it much more convenient to have up to date software
(that works, mind you.)

I have yet to try debian, mainly because I haven't been brave enough to
make the jump, and also because fundamentally, I want an operating
system that is easy to use and install.  (/me ducks for all the flames).

Any responses to these responses?

I am really looking for ideas to the age old question of how to get more
people involved.  I hope this prodding may lead to some action (on my
part, at the very least)

Ted





