Mine too.

>I don't know how this idea hit me, perhaps it's the virus talking (or
>the pain killers.)  I just thought that alpha doesn't get the respect it
>deserves and there are plenty of people on this list who could band
>together and make it much more convenient to have up to date software
>(that works, mind you.)
It didn't get respect for so long that Compaq gave it away to Intel.

>I have yet to try debian, mainly because I haven't been brave enough to
>make the jump, and also because fundamentally, I want an operating
>system that is easy to use and install.  (/me ducks for all the flames).
Not necessary to duck. I read the Deb lists. I can't say it is exactly an 
easy run over there.
But it will be REALLY NECESSARY, REALLY SOON.
After RH 7.2 I think it will likely be the only Alpha distribution that is 
still being developed.

As for getting more people involved I think it is now a matter of attrition.
With perhaps a few hundred new Alpha machines a month coming out to market 
at best..
And most of those in clusters, not on desktops, or small personal servers, 
I can't see where we are going to get new users from.

Alpha growth ended when Compaq bailed on developing new chipsets for small 
Alpha machines back in 1999.



