To the list:

I read an interesting article on Intel, AMD and their 64-bit CPUs.  The
writer thinks Intel's business model for Itanium is flawed and Hammer
could eat Intel's lunch.  You can find the article at:

www.theinquirer.net/09040201.htm

In this article, Intel's strategy for Itanium looks an awful lot like
DEC's Alpha strategy:  High price, specialized market, high margin.  AMD
in contrats is going for mass market right away with their fastest chip. 

My own thoughts on the future of Alpha is this:  there were an awful lot
of EV56 machines put out, hundreds of thousands of them.  The total
number of Alpha boxes out there has to be well over a million.  The EV6
machines are just now starting to show up on the used market ...  I
don't think shortage of hardware will be a problem for the Alph Linux
user community.  What might be a problem is shortage of current distros
and applications that take advantage of the Alpha.

If I were Intel, I would be trying to come up with a way to whomp up a
64-bit product that i could put out quickly, that was differentiated
from my flagship Itanium chip, and would run a Microsoft OS.  Hmm, lets
see if there is anything gathering dust in the closet ...

Well, that's my 2 cents on the subject.

jn


"Thursday"




