> From: "Peter Watkinson" <peterw871@netscapeonline.co.uk>
> Date: Sat, 20 Apr 2002 19:15:45 +0100
>
>  Maybe the Alpha was ahead of it's time and I guess there is a glimmer of
> hope if Intels markets really start to suffer that they'll resurect it or
> bring out some sort of High performance RISC cpu in the future.

Don't bet the rent money on Intel Corp. doing anything
beneficial to the progress of architectural or technical
progress.  (If anything, it might be worth betting
against that happening.)

In the mid-'80s, Intel Corp. had a superior 32-bit
architecture, the 960.  The architecture was designed to
be implemented at any of several levels, anywhere from
no floating point and no address translation all the way
to full floating point and object-based capability-based
fully protected addressing.  The first implementation was
roughly twice as fast as the 386, first tapeout only three
months after the 386.  Unfortunately, internal company
politics and short-term marketing schemes dictated death
by starvation for the architecture.

In a few years, when my Alpha is no longer competitive,
and/or no useful Linux distribution can be found, I suspect
my next processor will be an Intel.  I just hope it does
not have to be an X86.

Robert Riches
richesr1@inetarena.com




