On Fri, 19 Apr 2002, Ted Goodridge, Jr wrote:

>>>         I've been playing with FreeBSD on my LX164, there are some
>>> things I like about it, some I don't.  When I was looking at gentoo
>>> linux, I noticed they were adopting a bsd-style ports setup, but they
>>> don't support alpha...
>>> 
>>>         One thing that struck me about bsd is that the ports system
>>> allows patches to be applied to stock source to make the package run
>>> better on a specific os/arch type.
>
>> RPM allready does this. To both its benifit and detriment.
>
>To be completely honest, I have had many problems with RPM-based
>installs.  For example, suse and redhat both use rpm's, but put files in
>different places.  With RPM's, you have no control over where things go
>(as an end user)  Please, correct me if I am mistaken here.  RPM's don't
>fetch fresh source off a remote site using fetch like ports do.  All in
>all I like linux better, but RPM has some serious issues.

Red Hat is not able to maintain QA, if users randomly download
and compile stuff ala the ports system.  The QA guarantee ends
when the user recompiles any package, even if that package is
unmodified and built on a stock install.

If someone wants the perceived flexibility of the ports system, 
then Red Hat Linux is probably not the best distribution to meet 
their highly specialized needs.  Gentoo Linux does very much what 
you describe.  I wouldn't recommend using such a distribution on 
mission critical systems however, since modification of various 
compiler flags, etc. doesn't give one any guarantees of stability 
without adequate large scale beta test rollout/feedback/bugreport 
cycle, plus QA testing.

In short, it isn't supportable product to ship the distribution 
in a state where a user downloads all sources and rebuilds 
software optimized for their environment.


