On Fri, 19 Apr 2002, Peter Petrakis wrote:

>> What I'm really interested in with my initial post is whether or not
>> this is of interest to any of *YOU*.  Call it 'market research'. :)
>> Gentoo linux seems to be taking off, but there is no alpha port.
>
>It's certainly interesting. Part of research is exploring existing avenues
>to accomplish said goal ;-). Consider the distros that have signifigant
>presence on AXP. Suse, RH, and Debian, I know I'm not being P.C. here :-).
>
>maintainers of major packages i.e. gcc, binutils, X, and kernel absolutley
>MUST get fixes into upstream sources or else their patch tree becomes more
>than just a few fixes with some ditro tweaks but a whole different animal.

In the past, I have generally sent fixes into XFree86.org as I 
made them or found them.  Some fixes are applied right away, but 
most often only to the CVS head, and not the existing stable 
branch.  It depends on what all a given patch does however.

Due to the work overload of incoming patches however, they are 
often queued up at XFree86.org and don't get applied to CVS right 
away, rather they get applied just before the new release.  I 
think it would be better to apply a lot of them as they come in, 
so that they have wider spread testing prior to a final release, 
but I do not know all of the decision processes that go into it 
all, so I'm not going to second guess their processes myself.

This development cycle however, I've sent only a few patches 
upstream.  The rest are in my publically available src.rpm, and 
also extracted into individual patches on people.redhat.com.  I 
don't see the urgency of sending patches upstream right away if 
they are not going to be applied right away, and nobody else can 
see them publically AFAIK, so as long as I get them in before 
4.3.0 comes out, the effect is exactly the same to any end user 
or other developer.

It is not solely the responsibility of a package maintainer to 
get fixes into upstream sources.  It is also the responsibility 
of the upstream maintainer to apply the fixes that downstream 
vendors send them.  If there is no urgency to apply fixes, then 
there isn't really much urgency to send them right away.

FWIW, I would apply the fixes to CVS myself that I'm sure are 
safe to apply if I had CVS write access.


>Sending the patches to the package maintainers of the 'big three' ;-) has
>a better chance of getting the fix into upstream sources. I will not
>rank them.

For XFree86, patches should be sent to fixes@xfree86.org in 
addition to any vendors they're sent to.  This ensures that they 
have gotten the patches upstream, along with all of the 100 spam 
per day received at that address.  ;o)


