On Sat, 20 Apr 2002, Ted Goodridge, Jr wrote:

>So, who would be interested in keeping the alpha linux project alive?

Me.  ;o)

>The guys at redhat and suse and everywhere else have stated they are
>swamped.  What can we do as a community to help?

As I said in a previous mail, most developers here keep packages 
building on alpha and other architectures, even when there isn't 
specific project/product in line to do so.  The best thing anyone 
in the community can do to ensure decent packages continue to 
come out of the oven, is to not only file bug reports, but to 
attach bug fixes and patches to those bug reports.

If a bug report comes in on an architecture with no current 
project/product being planned for example, it is going to get a 
lot lower priority unless it also affects other architectures 
being developed.  Just a result of concentrating development 
effort on the products that pay our salaries, and allow us to 
continue developing.  If those bug reports come with bug 
fixes/patches however, that makes it a lot less workload for a 
developer to apply such fixes to a package, especially when it is 
more or less being done on personal time.

>My proposal is that instead of making these guys do it all themselves,
>we team up with them and come up with out own distro.  If that is not
>feasible, we need to do something to help out these overworked alpha
>gurus.

This is what is being done for Sparc right now via the Aurora 
project.  I don't see why Alpha or any other arch can't be 
assisted by the community as well.  Send in patches via bugzilla, 
and most likely the next package release will have them in it, or 
some release after that, assuming patches are sane.  This also 
applies to ia64/ppc/sparc and other arch's too.


>After all these posts, does anyone think we are closer to actually
>getting momentum to work on the underlying problem?

Probably.  All open source projects/programs proceed generally in
two ways.

1) A company or individual is paid for their work, and does the 
   work to meet the contractual obligations they've entered into, 
   and complete the work.

2) A company or individual has a personal interest in working on 
   a piece of code, and does it to scratch their own personal 
   itch or for other reasons that are important to them.

For #1, the company or individual is going to prioritize work to 
get the job done on schedule for which they're being paid.

For #2, the individual will prioritize various possible projects 
they are contributing to/interested on based on the time they 
have available, and their own personal preferences.

Also, in general, for #1, work gets done where it affects the 
most number of users.  Translated: Biggest bang for the buck.

Considering all of that, architecures other than ia32 usually do 
unfortunately take second hat by both open source developers, 
and also companies developing software.  Market forces are a 
reality that push software development efforts.

By the community helping out as much as possible for the things 
they want to see done, they're effectively becoming volunteer 
#2's, which helps greatly to keep things alive and kicking.  Also 
note that a lot of people who do work as #1's are also #2's too.

One way or another...  Long live Alpha!  ;o)



