On Sat, 20 Apr 2002, Maurice Hilarius wrote:

>With regards to your message at 05:18 PM 4/20/02, Mike A. Harris. Where you 
>stated:
>>Red Hat is not able to maintain QA, if users randomly download
>>and compile stuff ala the ports system.  The QA guarantee ends
>>when the user recompiles any package, even if that package is
>>unmodified and built on a stock install.
>So, for example, if one were to take the current Skipjack, and discover 
>that the binary for the i1000 driver is non-functional, and simply compile 
>from the sources on the set the same driver and it works properly, then the 
>QA has ended ??

You totally misunderstand what I've said.  Red Hat ships a 
product, and supports it.  In order for it to be supportable, 
there has to be common ground which has been tested.

When someone compiles something on their own, they introduce 
many variables into the equation.  What exact compiler version 
was used?  What version of binutils?  Were those packages also 
recompiled?  Were they recompiled with -march=i686 -O9 
-fultrafast-random-broken-compiler-option ?  Depending on what 
options an application was compiled with, the resultant binaries 
will be different.  Red Hat does not have the manpower to support 
every possible random build permutation.

Red Hat supports the binary product that it ships.  If a user 
recompiles anything, then that is something Red Hat did not ship, 
and does not support.

If you encounter a bug in something that is fixed by recompiling 
it, great.  File a bug report and indicate that the problem was 
solved by rebuilding it.  An engineer can then look into the 
problem and resolve it appropriately.

Quality assurance testing does NOT mean that a product makes it 
out the door with zero bugs.  It is not humanly possible to ship 
a Linux distribution with 1400+ packages most of which are 
externally written software, and be 100% bug free.  So yes, Red 
Hat Linux will have bugs.

Recompilation of software results in changing that software.  
That will affect the reproduceability of problems.  Having bug 
reports come in on a piece of software recompiled by the end 
user, can waste a LOT of engineering time trying to reproduce 
something which could have been introduced via changing compiler 
build options, or by different versions of libraries being 
installed than what was QA tested by Red Hat.

A build environment is a moving target.  Red Hat QA's packages 
produced in the Red Hat build environment, and not that of a 
random user's system.



>>In short, it isn't supportable product to ship the distribution
>>in a state where a user downloads all sources and rebuilds
>>software optimized for their environment.
>
>I agree that from a support point of view one has to be fairly strict, BTW..
>Still, nobody is perfect, so it strikes me that there has to be some level 
>of flexibility.

It is open source, so it is of course infinitely flexible.  One
is free to recompile anything they like, either from src.rpm,
from modifying the src.rpm's, or from upstream source tarballs
and the like.  Freedom to do this, is one of the things that
makes Linux an appealing platform.

That freedom however also comes with the responsibility to
resolve one's own problems when they do so.  Red Hat can't
support randomly built software outside the Red Hat build system.

Also, if someone thinks that it is possible to run an open source
company shipping a Linux distribution, and provide support for 
that which I claim above is unsupported, and try to turn a 
profit, they're also free to attempt such a venture.

It's all free software afterall.



