On Sat, 20 Apr 2002, Ted Goodridge, Jr wrote:

>Date: Sat, 20 Apr 2002 22:12:35 -0500
>From: "Ted Goodridge, Jr" <tedgoodridgejr@acm.org>
>To: axp-list@redhat.com
>Content-Type: text/plain;
>	charset="us-ascii"
>List-Id: Linux and Red Hat on Alpha processors <axp-list.redhat.com>
>Subject: RE: Interesting idea....(at least I think so)
>
>Ports are maintained by a maintainer.  The QA process is there, just
>less centralized.  Each port maintainer is responsible for patches and
>making sure it works.  It's just a different philosophy.  Usually a
>porter just makes sure its run and patches are applied, and that person
>works with the developer directly

I wasn't implying that no testing occurs in a system like ports 
or the like...  Rather, I am saying that when you increase 
variables, such as compiler options, varying installed 
applications, toolchain, libraries, you are going to get 
resultant binaries that differ.  There is no common ground.  What 
works on one persons system, might segfault on another persons 
system.  When tracked down, it could be because user B prefers to 
use -O6 passed to the compiler.  That bypasses the QA process 
entirely, because you can't say package foo works.

In the Red Hat build system, if a package is rebuilt with ZERO 
changes other than bump the release number and rebuild - that 
package has it's QA status set to UNTESTED.  A QA engineer must 
then fully retest that package with all automated and manual 
tests again.  Once tested it will be marked as such, and reset to 
untested if it is ever rebuilt.  Rebuilding any software 
invalidates any testing done with previous binaries since the 
variable environment in which that software was built may have 
changed.

It's not possible to provide assurance that rebuilding something 
on someone else's system will produce the exact same results as 
were QA tested.  So, while a user is free to do that since it is 
free software it is not supported.  If a bug is found in 
something after rebuilding it, it isn't considered a bug unless 
it can be reproduced in the binary software provided by Red Hat.
However, if such a bug can be fixed, and the fix doesn't itself 
introduce a new problem, generally the fix will be applied so the 
software becomes more robust.

I've personally received many bug reports from users that have 
rebuilt things themselves with different compiler options and the 
like.  I've spent hours of my own personal time, as well as Red 
Hat time troubleshooting problems users have had, but which I 
could not reproduce, only to find out much later on, that the 
problems only occured because they built with risky compiler 
optimizations, and went away when they removed those 
optimizations.

Scenarios like that would increase dramatically if the system
came installed ready to rebuild things uber-easily.  This would
raise the cost of research and development significantly for IMHO
very very little useful gain.  It is hard enough maintaining
software as it is than increasing the number of random variables.

So, while I certainly respect and encourage users to exercise the 
free software in any manner they like, I don't think that Red Hat 
should have to support things that were not shipped, and rebuilt 
software falls into that category.



-- 
