>> So, for example, if one were to take the current Skipjack, and discover
>> that the binary for the i1000 driver is non-functional, and simply
>> compile from the sources on the set the same driver and it works
>> properly, then the
>> QA has ended ??
>
>No. QA and the release manager have failed since the user should 'never'
>have been presented with that scenerio.

QA testing does not find 100% of all bugs.  QA testing does not 
remove all software flaws.  It raises the bar on quality by 
finding more bugs sooner, so they can hopefully be fixed.  There 
will always be bugs that do not get found in software regardless 
of how much QA testing goes on.

With respect to hardware, Red Hat does not have every single 
piece of hardware available, so we rely on users beta testing 
stuff and providing useful feedback, and bug reports.  Hopefully 
that i1000 (whatever it is) works.  Hopefully that i1000 doesn't 
come in 8 different chip revisions each of which behave 
differently, and the i1000 that was tested works, but the next 
revision does not.  Also, another factor is BIOS revisions, bad 
hardware, bad hardware combinations.  It is not possible to QA 
test all of this stuff or all permutations of this stuff.

If QA tests whizboard-pro on a machine and it works, but that 
same board plugged into the next PCI slot fails miserably for 
some reason.  Is that a QA failure?  Should QA add that to the 
battery of tests?  "Make sure every piece of hardware is tested 
in every motherboard we have, and in each PCI slot."

I don't think anyone sane would think that.  So someone claiming 
that QA testing sucks because something doesn't work for them in 
their setup, is just expecting pie-in-the-sky perfection that 
does not and never will exist.

QA testing is about raising the bar on quality, not being 100% 
flawless.  And our QA department does a fantastic job at that.



