Allen Ziegenfus wrote:

>What are RedHat's plans for alpha? On one hand, the FAQ for 7.2
>seems to say that work on alpha will only be done because of an
>outside interest like Compaq. At the same time, there are still new
>RawHide RPMS being created out there (like Mozilla 0.9.9).
>
>Is this a change from how things were done before, and if so why?
>I mean, I can understand them not wanting to fully support alpha due to
>constraints, but I didn't see any press release about this.
>
The way the build system here works is that you submit a src rpm to the 
build and it shedules the compilation on a list of available build 
boxes. Normally the default targets are i386, ia64 and alpha. As new 
rpms are created, the rawhide process sucks them in automagically and 
makes them available. The only time you won't see alpha is if a src rpm 
specfically excludes alpha (Excludearch: alpha) in the the spec file. 
That tends to happen when it won't build and the developer just doesn't 
want to spend time fixing the problem.

It's more a case of 'if it works, great, else Excludearch'


Phil
