--- Rob Byrnes <robbyrnes@ozemail.com.au> wrote:

> There is no need to splinter the Alpha using Linux
> community even 
> more.  Debian (to their credit) have released an
> Alpha version at the same 
> time as the i386 version since the Alpha port became
> stable, so why not 
> simply join an existing effort to make it stonger?
> 
> Cheers,
> Rob

I'll have to agree with this point.

It's clear that financial obligations for Red Hat and
SuSE make their respective Alpha ports precarious at
best and considering the size of the Linux/Alpha
userbase and ambivalence on Compaq's (management's)
part, we really ought not rely on continued commercial
support much longer.

Joining Debian has every advantage going for it - it's
a highly-respected distro and, most importantly, is
free from such financial and userbase considerations
and thus makes a more stable, secure mindshare
investment ;-) 
 
What's more, Debian is staunchly multi-arch, unlike
commercial distros (and some kernel maintainers) that
focus on i386 and PPC because of marketshare.

Otherwise I fear that those eschewing i386 and PPC
will soon have no choice but to move to NetBSD :-/

sousa

