> If the likelyhood of RH selling more alpha boxed sets and support is
> fast dwindling why not cut your losses. Both business and psychic. I am
> sure it will not be too long before debian follows suit with RH and cuts
> its support for the Alpha.

This is a 'very' good point. Though with regards to Debian, as long as it has
developers it will stay. They have a HURD distro you crying out loud!!!

> Yes there is a fine after market business in alphas but quite frankly
> other than there educational value I am not sure there is a lot of bang
> for the buck in getting an 164LX/SX over some commodity AMD motherboard.

I have to disagree here. The cheapest 64bit system you can purchase today is
from Sun, Blade 100. About $1200 and a $300 LX of EBAY will run circles around
it. Cheapest Itanium is 5K. So yeah if you compare apples to oranges nothing can
compete with the peecee world. Talk about machines in our class and we still
stand out. Though make no mistake. AMD's Hammer will 'not' be cheap when
compared to Athlon.  I suspect the chip will be 3x-5x the cost of the highest
priced Athlon and I havent even considered the mainboards. Which should put them
about on par with the blade 100 :-). They have to recoup R&D somehow and it
won't be from low margin commodity CPUS and flash chips.

> What the alpha community needs more than a new distribution is somebody
> to produce cost effective motherboards.  A daughter card that would plug
> into an X86 processor slot/socket might go a long way to breathing life
> into the Alpha.

Talk to Samsung. They are 'it'. As for x86 conversion. You 'may' be able to do
it with an Athlon based board. It would require some engineering resources
though. Nothing anyone could figure out in their basement ;-)

> I am sure that next year Compaq will be selling a number of alpha's that
> is very close to 0(zero) because who will want to buy a system that is
> going to be obsolete in a year. Mind you it may be a great chance to get

The ones who had to get the job done, yesterday. To them it's just a tool and
price is not an object. Hence Wildfire.

> some nice Compaq hardware cheap since they may have to give it away to
> get rid of it.  I once had the opertunity to be with a company that
> allowed the sales staff to start selling the new system before it was
> ready. They had to resort to giving out the old systems and promising to
> replace them with the new ones when the new ones were available. The
> company still exists but the people who bought it and restructured it
> dropped all the hardware.  Who remembers Osborne???

Interesting story, Never heard of the company before though...

> The real question is: Is there anything that can be done to resurect the
> alpha not which distribution will ride the train over the edge the
> longest.

Alpha is EOL as far as Compaq is concerned. Samsung is left. All you capitalists
out there, remember deskstation? Now make a business plan that works :-).

Peter
