> -----Original Message-----
> From: axp-list-admin@redhat.com [mailto:axp-list-admin@redhat.com]On
> Behalf Of Peter Petrakis
> stand out. Though make no mistake. AMD's Hammer will 'not' be cheap when
> compared to Athlon.  I suspect the chip will be 3x-5x the cost of 
> the highest
> priced Athlon and I havent even considered the mainboards. Which 
> should put them
> about on par with the blade 100 :-). They have to recoup R&D 
> somehow and it
> won't be from low margin commodity CPUS and flash chips.

	On the contrary, from what I've been reading, they are going
	to be VERY price competitive. They aren't going to take the
	Alpha model of recouping the investment over 1000 chips. It'll
	be over millions. They are pushing Hammer into everything from
	8-ways to laptops. They are doing it in a .09 process eventually.
	And the die size in .13 isn't much bigger than Athlon!

	Mind you, there will be two types. Clawhammer and Sledgehammer.
	The difference being cache and HyperTransport pin-outs. Clawhammer
	should be very competitive against P4 class prices. Sledgehammer
	is for servers. 

	I fully expect to see a Clawhammer CPU and motherboard costing
	around $250-300 by this time next year. AMD has a BIG chance to 
	leapfrog Intel. They aren't going to blow it. (Jerry Sanders 
	comments at the Microsoft trial not withstanding!)

	The days of a low-cost Alpha are behind us. Low cost Alpha's are
	what you find on Ebay.  IF you could come up with some sort of
	daughter/mezzanine card that plugged an Alpha into an Athlon MB,
	it would be expensive and probably bug ridden and would take too
	long to get out the door. Plus, you have a limited market. How will
	you compete against a Clawhammer running at 2+ GHz for $250-300?

								mike> 





