--- "Mike A. Harris" <mharris@redhat.com> wrote:

> 
> Your comment on "... we really ought not rely on
> continued 
> commercial support" seems to imply that the
> available solutions 
> out there that come without commercial support are
> inadequate or 
> you'd already be using them.
> 

Not at all; in fact I find that solutions with
non-commercial support (this
list/usenet/linuxalpha.org) are more than adequate and
in fact quite excellent. I use these solutions and
support daily at work and at home.

My comment merely suggests that commercially supported
solutions for unpopular archs are precarious at best
and that this small community ought not rely on them.

We must be aware that there are far more i386 and PPC
users in comparison to Alpha, SPARC, m68k etc.  A
commercial entity normally has fiduciary duties to its
investors and that unfortunately may jeopardize an
unpopular project despite the best of intentions.

That particular difference between a
commercially-supported solution and a strictly
non-profit user/developer-supported solution is what I
was attempting to highlight.

sousa

PS - It is notable that NetBSD supports a wide gamut
of archs while maintaining a very high level of
quality and consistency.  It appears that Debian has
the same aspirations.

