On Thu, 25 Apr 2002, Peter Petrakis wrote:

> I'd like to see how well the hypertransport memory bus scales.

Ditto.

> Also the compiler tech is questionable. gcc is not supposed to be
> 'fast', is AMD going to produce a optimized compiler?

Actually, gcc is supposed to be as fast as possible while remaining
portable (i.e. nothing that would break other archs or introduces odd
arch- or processor-specific pathways through the compiler).  I think they
can accomplish this.  Granted, gcc is usually not as fast as a
proprietary, binary-only compiler, but it usually comes pretty close and
is often times much more standards compliant than the proprietary
compilers :-)


> With regards to another post, without the 'killer app' it
> doesnt matter how cheap AMD produces hammer.
> It'll just be another Alpha in market history.

I think that will depend.  The x86 world has been hearing about 64-bit
architectures for awhile, but before, it meant taking a nosedive off of a
comfortable diving board (dumping current software and going with another
that runs on the 64-bit systems OR needing to maintain several different
licenses for the different platforms).  While the latter may never change,
they will probably feel more comfortable going from the x86 arch to the
x86-64 arch simply because it's named similarly (yes, people tend to be
this simple).  I don't think Hammer needs a killer app at all, especially
now that Microsoft has committed to 64-bit computing (for real this time,
since it doesn't involve a totally different platform produced by
someone other than AMD or Intel...aka "we can stay on familiar
ground").  If anything WinXP-64 could qualify as the "killer app" that
both Itanic and Hammer are looking for.  And, as unfortunate as it may be,
if Microsoft says it's ok, most businesses will tend to adopt it more
readily than if they hadn't put any work into porting things to it.

Besides, in the Windows server world, I would expect Hammer to fit in
quite nicely, from an architecture and pricing standpoint.  And, I know,
the whole world isn't Windows, but currently, the money goes where Win
does and that means AMD can move more units.

C





