On Thu, 25 Apr 2002, Peter Petrakis wrote:

>Date: Thu, 25 Apr 2002 10:02:33 -0400
>From: Peter Petrakis <voodoo@alphadriven.org>
>To: axp-list@redhat.com
>Content-Type: text/plain;
>	charset="iso-8859-1"
>List-Id: Linux and Red Hat on Alpha processors <axp-list.redhat.com>
>Subject: RE: The AMD 64 bit future now official
>
>I'd like to see how well the hypertransport memory bus scales. Also the compiler
>tech is questionable. gcc is not supposed to be 'fast' , is AMD going to produce
>a optimized compiler? With regards to another post, without the 'killer app' it
>doesnt matter how cheap AMD produces hammer.
>It'll just be another Alpha in market history.

Very unlikely.  The Alpha was not ever targeted at home user 
market or desktop.  The Hammer/Opteron is.  It will replace the 
current Athlon family as the next generation CPU when it comes 
out, and will be targetted directly at the consumer market.  This 
has been touted for quite some time.  The Opteron (Sledgehammer) 
is targetted at 2-8way servers, however the Clawhammer version, 
which is targetted at 1-2way machines, will keep the Athlon 
monicker.  All of this information is on AMD's public web pages, 
and documentation.

Also, aparently Microsoft is backing the chip aparently, and 
Intel's own semi-secret "Yamhill" CPU will use AMD's instruction 
set as well under a cross-licensing agreement.

Some of that info is what I found out via google today, both on 
AMD's website, as well as various other sites which have had 
previews of the chip et al.

The Hammer will be here to stay IMHO, of course time will tell.


