----- Original Message -----
From: "Ted Goodridge, Jr" <tedgoodridgejr@acm.org>
Subject: RE: What have we learned? Debian....


> What is the underlying reason debian hasn't gone to 2.4.x?
>
> Ted

The "Debian way" is to make a release at a particular point in time, and
then only roll out security or critical functionality updates by backporting
code to the released version (if possible).  No new code is released for a
given version (that is - no new versions of applications are released as
part of Debian Release)

Kinda difficult to understand - but it does make Debian quite possibly the
most stable Linux distro around.

Kernel 2.4.18 should be the released kernel for Debian 3.0 (the Woody
release) when 3.0 becomes Stable, and the next release after that becomes
Testing.  I suppose an explanation is in order.  Debian is released in
cycles .. Unstable, Testing and Stable.  These cycles have different
versions moving through them, Unstable is always called Sid (Sid breaks
toys), while Testing and Stable change.  The current release of Debian
(2.2r6) is codenamed Potato, while the version curently in testing is called
Woody.  (yes, Debian uses characters from Toy Story as codenames)

Cheers,
Rob

