I understand you are frustrated.  An emotional state of serious
frustration is obvious from the tone of the above complaints.
How about if you deal with the emotional state on your own, and
use the mailing list to deal with the technical issues and try to
solve them.

The first thing that will help us help you is a little bit of
useful information.  What hardware are you running: system type,
motherboard type, processor type, number of processors, amount of
memory, type of memory, types of plug-in cards, types of disks?
What system software are you running: which distribution, what
version of that distribution, what version of this Gaussian98
program, what services you have enabled/disabled, what other
software or activities on the system?  What are the
characteristics of the freezes and/or crashes?  Is there anything
useful in logs?  Have you tried any type of memory or other
hardware diagnostics?

If you have been following the list, you have seen recent
discussion of hardware problems causing crashes.  You might
consider checking a few of the suggestions offered in that
thread.  Or, perhaps this Gaussian98 program you are running is
doing something unusual that happens to tickle a vulnerable spot
in the kernel.

Do the crashes/freezes _only_ happen when running Gaussian98, or
do they happen even when doing nothing or doing something else?
Is there a particular thing Gaussian98 does that seems related to
the crashes/freezes?

You might consider running Gaussian98 under strace.  That may get
you close to finding out what it does that might be causing the
problem.  You might consider throwing the output from strace to a
serial port or over a network to try to get the last (and most
important) messages.

In case it will help inspire a little hope, Linux and Alpha are
normally a very robust platform.  My home system is subject to a
great deal of varied use.  Current uptime is 22 days.  The last
time it was rebooted, it was up to 33 days.  I seem to recall it
was up to 49 days once.  Most reboots have been due to me needing
to work on the system, not for crashes or freezes.  I have seen
reports of other Linux systems achieving uptimes much longer than
I have quoted for my system.

Good luck.

Robert Riches
richesr1@inetarena.com




