I am definitely not an expert on kernel memory allocation, but it
appears to me that the messages say it's a kernel bug, even
showing the file name and line number where the problem is
detected: file=page_alloc.c, line=240.  If you're running a
Redhat kernel, I'd say that looks like fair game to file a bug,
unless it has already been reported.  Look at this URL to check
and/or file:

    http://bugzilla.redhat.com/bugzilla

There are also non-Redhat kernel bug lists which you could check
and/or file a report to.

Do you have any swap space enabled?  Look at /proc/swaps to see
what you have enabled--just "cat" or "more" that file.  Changing
(or enabling) swap space arrangements might help work around or
solve this problem.  With disk space as cheap as it currently is,
with a workload of as significant a size as yours, it would
probably be good to give the system at least a few GB of swap
partition to play with.  A swap partition is better than a swap
file, but a swap file is much better than nothing.

Another possible workaround/solution would be to try more RAM,
if that is a possibility.  Using 580MB of a total 768MB of RAM
for just one job is getting close to a level that might cause
non-trivial paging, especially if there's anything else going
on, such as other background processes, users logged in, an X
server, etc.  More RAM would be fairly likely to give you better
performance, too, if it reduces paging activity.

In summary, I think we have found the smoking gun: an apparent
kernel bug.  If it were me, I'd check (and enable) swap space,
then try to find a kernel patch/fix or more RAM.

Good luck.

Robert Riches
richesr1@inetarena.com




