On Sun, Apr 28, 2002 at 01:54:14PM -0700, Stephen Williams wrote:
> 
> michal@ellpspace.math.ualberta.ca said:
> > If you will try to do that then either you have to tell rpm not to
> > bother with missing "provides" of 'kernel-drm = 4.2.0' or redo your
> > own rpm with this.
> 
> It's easy enough to install the update kernel and not use it. That
> satisfies the dependencies,

Read again.  It does not.  Unless you will redo things yourself none of
kernel rpm packages for Alpha, either from updates or from rawhide, has
a 'kernel-drm = 4.2.0' provide.  Yes, you can make one with it.

> I really loath using the --nodeps option, and prefer to get all
> the needed packages onto the rpm -Fvh command line.

Simple '-Fvh' will not work in this case as there are new packages,
like 'XFree86-base-fonts' - for example, which will be not picked
that way.  Something pretty close - yes; but unfortunately there is
no good way to tell "-Fvh and I want these extra packages too".
Crucial programs like 'mkfontdir' are now in 'XFree86-font-utils'
and this will be not updated by '-Fvh' too.

> Someone (maybe
> the find-requires program) went through considerable effort to
> put those dependencies there so that I (the user in this case)
> don't need to guess.

In this case you have to guess because you are doing things which
go quite a bit outside of an "official and supported" distribution.
After a fact but '--verify' option can tell you if did things right.
Or you may wait until a distro with such support will show up.
This may be a while.

> My question, therefore, was more whether I could to the install
> of the 4.2.0 rpms and still be able to back out if they don't do
> what needs to be done.

The answer to that is "yes" as well (and this is not only a theory :-)
but you better keep a record what you changed between "before" and
"after".  It is quite simple if you are paying attention.

Unfortunately totally de-installing and putting from scratch another
version of X is not a realistic option.  Many things depend on X
libraries so you will have long dependency chains.  Checking
dependencies first, with '-Fvh' :-), and working them out, is much
simpler and even safer.

  Michal




