Hi,
The kernel you're using seems to be 2.4.9...
Some time in our life all we feel so fustrated as you
don't worry! just try to deal with your problems!
I have here several alpha running both gaussian
and linux. I'm using linux RH6.2 because i simply can't
turn off the machines to reinstall a more recent version :-)
Currently i'm using kernel 2.2.17.

Some of the machines have uptimes for more than 200 days (perhaps
the last visit of the cleaning lady :-) i'm talking about PWS xp100
and 600au.
Gaussian is mostly writen in fortran and it uses 32 bits for the int
but it doesn't means that gaussian will not run on a 64 bits machine!
and to be honest i don't bealive that gaussian will disturb the kernel.
two or three years ago i had a similar problem as yours, with one SGI
we have here. The calculation just stoped and a message apeared on the
kernel logs saying that link xxx was not found or something like "was
not loaded into memory" (i don't recal the message but it was something
to do with this)... you can't imagine what was said here about the machine!

Well there's even a long story but the point is that this people here was
filling up the disk, and all the memory of the machine was being used!
When the SO tried to swap some memory page it simply didn't have a place
to put it (don't ask me about swap partitons on SGI because i know nothing
about it), as it couldn't free some memory to load the link xxx (gaussian calls
links to their subrutines) the calculation stoped!

As i told you off the list your gaussian input file seems to be ok (i asked someone
here for that).
What i suggest is:
try to increase memory on your machine;
try to make a bigger swap partition;

nelson

