>   From: "Christopher C. Chimelis" <chris@debian.org>
>   Date: Thu, 25 Apr 2002 13:20:59 -0400 (EDT)
>
>   On Thu, 25 Apr 2002, Peter Petrakis wrote:
>
>   > Also the compiler tech is questionable. gcc is not supposed to be
>   > 'fast', is AMD going to produce a optimized compiler?
>
>   Actually, gcc is supposed to be as fast as possible while remaining
>   portable (i.e. nothing that would break other archs or introduces odd
>   arch- or processor-specific pathways through the compiler).  I think they
>   can accomplish this.  Granted, gcc is usually not as fast as a
>   proprietary, binary-only compiler, but it usually comes pretty close and
>   is often times much more standards compliant than the proprietary
>   compilers :-)
>
What did Compaq actually open-source over the past summer, just before the
HP announcement? 

I had heard one story that certain compiler technology related to Alpha
code generatoin was being open-sourced. However, I had no details on what 
that might have really meant, or if it really happened. [There was some
talk about a year ago of Compaq enhancing gcc to generate beter Alpha code,
instead of maintaining their own compiler suite.]

>   > With regards to another post, without the 'killer app' it
>   > doesnt matter how cheap AMD produces hammer.
>   > It'll just be another Alpha in market history.
>
>   I think that will depend.  The x86 world has been hearing about 64-bit
>   architectures for awhile, but before, it meant taking a nosedive off of a
>   comfortable diving board (dumping current software and going with another
>   that runs on the 64-bit systems OR needing to maintain several different
>   licenses for the different platforms).  While the latter may never change,
>   they will probably feel more comfortable going from the x86 arch to the
>   x86-64 arch simply because it's named similarly (yes, people tend to be
>   this simple).  

Sometime this year (already?) Intel will be releasing the Compaq based
compilers for the Itanium devlopers.  Part and parcel of that deal was
apparently the 32-64 bit "porting guides" expertise from DEC (etc... to
Alpha) 

It seems to be that, at least so far, the Alpha invasion of Intel has in
fact been surprisingly successful. I'm hearing rumors that the post
Deerfield chip will be radically different from those which preceed it, and
in the 2004 time frame.  But until then, almost everything will beat IPF...
especially the PowerPC.

>   I don't think Hammer needs a killer app at all, especially
>   now that Microsoft has committed to 64-bit computing (for real this time,
>   since it doesn't involve a totally different platform produced by
>   someone other than AMD or Intel...aka "we can stay on familiar
>   ground").  If anything WinXP-64 could qualify as the "killer app" that
>   both Itanic and Hammer are looking for.  And, as unfortunate as it may be,
>   if Microsoft says it's ok, most businesses will tend to adopt it more
>   readily than if they hadn't put any work into porting things to it.
>
Remember, unless Microsoft dumped all of its engineers over about 25, there
are at least a couple of them who worked on NT on the Alpha... I would
assume that XP is far more 64-bit-clean than we might suspect. Who know, it
might already run on an Alpha in Bill's closet, and the port to Hammer,
becomes simply an exercise in hackery... [Don't forget -- Apple's OSX ran
on Alpha since early in the Public Beta even though it's not officially a
64 bit clean OS. I was told that the port took only a couple of days.]

Forbes magazine, last issue or 2 issues back was also speculating on how AMD
might Hammer on Intel in the CPU field, as well as how IBM was going to be
hammering Intel on the ASIC side.

The real jist of that article is that the Pentium chips come in around $500
each while the true volume chips (like for cell phones) come in around $5!
That generates a BIG squeeze on margins when the price of your primary
money-maker gets cut in half.

It's interesting, but today, Intel has an architecture story that looks a
lot like Digital of old... far too many incompatible product lines which
appear to be "cost effective" and "self sustaining," but which are likely
to come up very short in the margin arena. And, like Digital, customers
will be expected to CHANGE from one architecture to another as they move up
from desktop to department to enterprise.

I still maintain that by 2005, the Computing Market will have a very wide
"split." The low end machines, and I do mean your home PC here,  will be
equivalent to EV7s and priced in the $100 range. The high end machines
will be serious Marvel class machines.... and there won't be anything in
the middle.

-- 
